+# JSRF v17 D3D fence stall — root cause and v18 correction
+
+## What v17 fixed
+
+v17 fixed the `lock xadd` flag-tracking bug that had caused a COM object to be
+destroyed twice and the retail heap large free list to self-link. The v17 run
+no longer spins in `sub_001497DC`; it reaches 3,915 indirect calls and enters
+the retail D3D shutdown path.
+
+## v17 watchdog evidence
+
+The final decoded guest call chain is:
+
+```
+0x00154FD0 -> 0x0018CDF0 -> 0x001925A0 -> 0x00191710 -> 0x00191440
+```
+
+The final watchdog registers are:
+
+```
+eax=00000002 ecx=DEADBEEF edx=80000000
+edi=00000007 esi=21524118
+```
+
+There are no kernel calls during the final 60-second watchdog interval, so the
+stall is guest CPU/memory polling rather than a kernel wait.
+
+## Retail D3D code that explains the hang
+
+Retail `0x00191710` calls `0x00191440`. In `0x00191440`, the tight wait is:
+
+```asm
+001914E2  mov edx,[edi+34h]
+001914E5  mov edi,[edi+30h]
+001914E8  mov eax,edi
+001914EA  sub eax,esi
+001914F0  mov ecx,[edx]
+001914F2  mov esi,edi
+001914F4  sub esi,ecx
+001914F6  cmp eax,esi
+001914F8  jb  001914F0
+```
+
+The watchdog register tuple is exactly this loop:
+
+- `edx = 0x80000000` — the guest-visible GPU completion/fence address.
+- `ecx = 0xDEADBEEF` — the current value read from that address.
+- `edi = 7` — the submitted/PUT-side count from `device + 0x30`.
+- `esi = 7 - 0xDEADBEEF = 0x21524118`.
+- `eax = 2` — the comparison window retained by the retail routine.
+
+Therefore `jb 0x001914F0` remains true indefinitely while the completion value
+never advances.
+
+## Why 0x80000000 contains DEADBEEF
+
+Retail D3D itself allocates a 96-byte contiguous block and stores its pointer in
+`device + 0x34`. It initializes that first dword to 3 in `0x00191020`, then
+later deliberately writes the debug/synchronization sentinel:
+
+```asm
+0019247E  mov dword ptr [80000000h],0DEADBEEFh
+```
+
+On real Xbox hardware, submitted NV2A work subsequently updates this GPU-visible
+completion/semaphore word. A static recompiler without a real NV2A consumer
+must provide the equivalent completion acknowledgement or the retail wait is
+correctly infinite.
+
+## Existing xboxrecomp mechanism
+
+Pinned xboxrecomp already contains `xbox_Nv2aMirrorFence(device_ptr_va,
+put_off, get_ptr_off)` specifically for this hardware-semantic gap. Its worker
+follows the registered chain and mirrors the title's submitted count into the
+GPU completion word:
+
+```
+device = MEM32(device_ptr_va)
+fence  = MEM32(device + get_ptr_off)
+MEM32(fence) = MEM32(device + put_off)
+```
+
+The runtime comments describe the same pattern: D3D seeds a semaphore in
+contiguous memory, submits work, then spins until the GPU-written value reaches
+the submitted count. Because this runtime treats submitted work as completed,
+mirroring the count is its intended hardware acknowledgement.
+
+## v18 correction
+
+JSRF's retail offsets are proven directly by `0x00191440`:
+
+- device pointer global: `0x0019DCE0`
+- submitted count / PUT-side value: `device + 0x30`
+- pointer to GPU completion word: `device + 0x34`
+
+v18 therefore registers exactly:
+
+```c
+xbox_Nv2aMirrorFence(0x0019DCE0u, 0x30u, 0x34u)
+```
+
+This is host hardware emulation using xboxrecomp's existing generic mechanism;
+it does not bypass the retail wait, patch the retail D3D function, or substitute
+game logic.
+
+v18 also keeps a read-only `D3DWAIT` probe at the exact retail wait landmarks,
+including support for the contiguous guest window `0x80000000..0x83FFFFFF`, so
+the next Windows run can verify the fence progresses and identify the next
+actual blocker if one remains.
+
+## Separate issue intentionally not bundled
+
+v17 also shows repeated `NtFreeVirtualMemory` failures returning
+`STATUS_UNSUCCESSFUL`. That is a distinct bridge correctness issue and is not
+changed in v18. It should be addressed independently if it remains observable
+after the D3D fence stall is removed.
